        "id": 11715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-31T23:05:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I get what you are doing, but no, we shouldn\u0027t put the auth methods in a thing called \"Error\". Why not make this a ServerAuthCallbacks?",
      "revId": "aedddc98c6687c1e99c576010ff42ea0e00f02c5",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "56b2d94a_b3b4c25d",
        "filename": "ssh/server.go",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 527,
      "author": {
        "id": 11715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-31T23:05:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we need to support changing user halfway a multi-step authentication? It\u0027s more likely to cause bad bugs (what if we later set some other field of s based on userAuthReq.User and forget to reset it here?) than to be useful.\n\nLet\u0027s have a way to know if we are past a partial success (you can also use it in place of keeping the noClientAuthAllowed variable) and in that case error out if the user changes.\n\nAlso allows moving the initial ServerAuthCallbacks setup outside the loop, which is clearer.\n\nP.S. There\u0027s also a risk an application will save ConnMetadata.User() in the PartialSuccessError closure. Let\u0027s definitely error out.",
      "revId": "aedddc98c6687c1e99c576010ff42ea0e00f02c5",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705"
    },
